#!/usr/bin/env python3

"""Roll every measurement of a model into one file: data/usecase.json.

What is this model FOR is a question the corpus can mostly answer with numbers,
and this collects them in one place so a consumer does not have to re-derive
the joins. Each entry carries:

  facets      one number per (source, benchmark), with the model's percentile
              among the registry models that also carry it, and a reference
  languages   gbench per-language success rates, which is the only source here
              that says a model is better at rust than at clojure
  community   what each forum says, kept per forum, with quoted references
  assessed    a written judgement, carried in from usecase-assessed.json.
              that file is SOURCE and this one is derived, so a recompute
              cannot lose the prose and a diff shows judgement changing
              separately from measurement. it is machine-readable on purpose:
              a generated MODELS.md should read it rather than a document

Facets are deliberately not blended here. Weighing coding against community
approval is the reader's call, so the viewer applies weights at read time and
this file keeps the components separable. `derived.best_for` is the exception:
a cheap top-third summary so a consumer with no weighting UI still gets an
answer.

Every number keeps a `ref` -- source, file and url. A measurement nobody can
trace is an assertion, and the whole point of this repo is that a fact has a
place it came from.

    ./analyze-usecase                # rewrite data/usecase.json
    ./analyze-usecase --check        # exit 1 if it is out of date
    ./analyze-usecase --missing      # models with no assessed block yet

The joins (gbench, lmarena, swe-rebench, card claims) are imported from
build-tables rather than restated, because two definitions of "this model's
gbench score" is exactly the drift this repo exists to remove.
"""

